{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792351278,"nonce":"Ax3eFyD2TbKviZzMsduEjBt5+SM=","title":"Frameless window with a custom title bar and simulated window controls, styled per platform","message":"Requested by the user, 2026-10-18 (queued, not started): the RayDE window has no system frame. RayDE draws the title bar and the close, minimize and fullscreen/maximize controls itself, in the style of each platform.\n\nWhat the window layer already gives (checked in hlwindow and ashui's WindowConfig, so no ask of ashui is expected): `decorations(false)`, `Window.dragWindow()`, `dragResizeWindow(direction)`, `setMinimized`, `setMaximized`, `setFullscreen` and `isMinimized`, `isMaximized`, `isFullscreen`, `scaleFactor`, `buttons(close, minimize, maximize)`.\n\nPlan\n- Open the window with decorations off. The title bar (Workbench.titleBar, 40 px, the project pill in the middle) becomes the drag area: pointer down on empty bar calls dragWindow, a double click toggles maximize (zoom on macOS), and the controls and the pill do not start a drag.\n- Resize by the edges: a 5 px border and the four corners call dragResizeWindow with the matching direction, with the resize cursors; none while maximized or fullscreen.\n- Controls, one component per style, chosen from the platform, with `appearance.windowControls` in .rayde/settings.json (auto, macos, windows, linux) and a choice in the Appearance section:\n  - macOS: three 12 px circles on the left (close red, minimize yellow, fullscreen green), glyphs only while the pointer is over the group, grey when the window is not in front; green toggles fullscreen, Option+green maximizes.\n  - Windows: minimize, maximize or restore, close on the right, 46 by 32 px, flat; hover tints, close turns red; glyphs drawn as SVG.\n  - Linux: round buttons on the right (GNOME style), with the side as a setting, and a flat style for KDE; picked from XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP when auto.\n- Commands and keys: Minimize (Cmd+M), Toggle Full Screen (Ctrl+Cmd+F, F11), Maximize; shown in the command center and the keymaps.\n- Corners and shadow: the window outline needs rounded corners on macOS and Linux (transparent window with the shadow drawn, or the system's), checked per platform; Windows keeps square corners.\n- Accessibility: tooltips and labels on the controls; the controls are focusable and respond to Enter and Space.\n- Unfocused look: needs the window's focus events; confirm they reach ashui.\n\nKnown risks\n- A frameless macOS window loses the system's fullscreen animation and the traffic lights' native behaviour unless the title bar is made transparent with full-size content; the user asked for simulated controls, so check what hlwindow can do and fall back to calling setFullscreen directly.\n- Windows 11 snap layouts on the maximize button need a native hit test; simulated controls cannot offer them.\n- Wayland: dragResizeWindow and dragWindow need compositor support; test on X11 and Wayland.\n\nDone when: the window opens frameless on macOS, Windows and Linux with the right controls on the right side; the bar drags the window and a double click maximizes it; the edges resize it; each control works and the maximize glyph follows the state; snapshots show the bar in every style and both themes; the style can be chosen in Settings.","files":null}]}